{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1376969-real-life-history-controversies","id":1376969,"title":"Real Life History controversies","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-03-28T08:59:10-07:00","id":1376969,"title":"Real Life History controversies","content":"Like topic states. My controversial opinion - <b>Renaissance started in 9th century Byzantine Empire.</b> Seeing how Italian city-states of 15th century were based on long cultural and political relation with Constantinople. And how New Rome was heart of cultural heritage of Greek and Roman civilizations. It seems to come from logical implications.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 03-28-2020, 06:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-28T13:37:32-07:00","id":1376990,"title":"","content":"Do you plan on elaborating on this post? It's a little vague. Haha","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26060-nuhxnger36","name":"NuHxnger36","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26060_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-28T15:54:19-07:00","id":1377004,"title":"","content":"I mean that most of what we think about Renaissance as period - have it's roots in 7th till 10th century Byzantine Empire. Let's see on the Renaissance main elements:<br /> <br /> 1. <b>Humanism</b> - Eastern Roman Empire was known to sustain network of state run Universities. People were important for the state government.<br /> 2. <b>Greek and Roman civilization legacy</b> - Byzantium was officially known as Eastern Roman Empire, and it's citizens were still believing they are descendent of ancient Romans - Even when they officially speak Greek and being in their and Asia Minor lands. Universal education was based on models of Hellenized world and law of the Empire was direct continuation of it from Rome's times.<br /> 3. <b>Secularism</b> - Here Byzantium is counter to the thesis as it's practically described as 'first Christian Empire', being de facto Theocracy of (future) Orthodox Church.<br /> 4. <b>Renaissance Italy was build on Byzantine ashes</b> - Genoese and Venetians were in very closed relations with Eastern Romans. Venice has started as Byznatine colony in Italy, even. When Constantinopole fell, many of it's scholars and works of culture of Roman and Greek world traveled to those city-states.<br /> <br /> I'm preparing to run <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> game on Roll20 about Byzantine period - and for the game, I needed to have some ideas of visuals about people, things and general feel of the scenes. Thinking about it, I came to my thesis conclusion - that Byzantine Empire is like Renaissance 500-800 years earlier. With that, I can use Renaissance assets and elements, when cannot find proper ones from the original time period.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 03-28-2020, 06:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-28T20:00:45-07:00","id":1377050,"title":"","content":"What specifically do you suggest kickstarted the Renaissance? Because it also sounds like the Byzantium also inherited the Renaissance via this paradigm of thought.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-28T23:02:45-07:00","id":1377100,"title":"","content":"<b>Elgagusto</b>, you have some point here. Most of historian of previous generation - at least in Poland - say that Middle Ages were 'Dark Ages'. You know, nothing was invented, people were locked in their cities behind walls, religion was over present. And, partially, they are right. General population lived by that. Only the universities became the harbors of those ideas start by Greeks and Romans, that slowly returned to the world. In West, local Universities started to show up in 10th and 11th century, Before them, there were only higher schools build by Byznatines in Italy ( in Venice, Eastern Romans colony, for example ) and those by Muslims ( that started as rebelled provinces in Byzantine Empire that joined new religion - Islam - in 6th and 7th century ). It's all the great chain of learning, streaming from Greece and Rome.<br /> <br /> Wikipedia on the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medieval_university\" target=\"_blank\">Medieval University</a> - 'Some scholars such as Syed Farid Alatas have noted some parallels between Madrasahs and early European colleges and have thus inferred that the first universities in Europe were influenced by the Madrasahs in Islamic Spain and the Emirate of Sicily.Other scholars such as George Makdisi, Toby Huff and Norman Daniel, however, have questioned this, citing the lack of evidence for an actual transmission from the Islamic world to Christian Europe and highlighting the differences in the structure, methodologies, procedures, curricula and legal status of the &quot;Islamic college&quot; (madrasa) versus the European university.'<br /> <br /> Revolution of Renaissance period (15th century) is that is was <b><i>massive</i></b>. When Constantinople fell, Universities were already popular in Europe - and then next wave of the teachers from New Rome come to them. In Byzantine Empire period ( up to 11th century ) you had 5 Universities and more or less homogeneous education culture stretch in Greece and Asia Minor. Western Europe, in that time, was divided in various petty kingdoms that needed to 'remember' they need higher education for existence of Empire. Even Charlemagne did not 'know' this, yet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-28T23:38:53-07:00","id":1377109,"title":"","content":"I'm not surprised that Charlemagne was ignorant of this, from my understanding he was illiterate but even he valued literacy. Okay so the inexorable factor that makes the Renaissance is the spread of institutions of higher education? What kind of Chronicles game are you going to run by the way? Just curious what kind of group is tackling the time period.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-29T00:21:20-07:00","id":1377115,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eldagusto</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1376969-real-life-history-controversies?p=1377109#post1377109\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What kind of Chronicles game are you going to run by the way? Just curious what kind of group is tackling the time period.</div> </div> </div> </div>Really, it's 'just second season' of our <b>Werewolf the Forsaken</b> Vikings game in summer of year 800, few months before crowning of Charlemagne. Empress <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irene_of_Athens\" target=\"_blank\">Irene of Athens</a> is de facto ruler of Byzantium and here son is officially bed-ridden in palace. Unofficially, there are rumors Empress murdered her own son to take throne. My bloody Norse players finally got from Uppsala to Constantinopole. They come to seek solution for problems in Uppsala - showing of Geryo ( Spiritual ) Dragon from Eastern Europe in their lands - and heard it came from 'Greece'. On their way, one of them got strucked by some mysterious illness - my <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1304111-contagion-fan-your-outbreaks?p=1304126#post1304126\">Iconclasm Contagion</a> - that if they uncover, was reason why Geryo left Greece in first place. It may sound simple, but we already have in play important questions of persons religions ( Norse, Christian, ancient Greece Polytheists ) and value of education. It will be interesting to put Norse as 'first Vikings in New Rome' in this context. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> / Yeah, I know first evidence show Vikings were in year 820 in Constantinopole - let's just let it run as Licentia Poetica of my game, I started it long before I found out this minor mistake. ( It's game I run now three years in April of 2020. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />) /<br /> <br /> I also incorporate a lot of <b>Contagion Chronicles</b> stuff - with using Sworn factions to show some crossovers NPCs, centering their backgrounds on Justinian Plague ( Black Death ) and now fights with Iconoclasm Contagion secretly spreading in Empire. More you can read on those on <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1137216-dark-eras-fan-byzantine-empire?p=1304030#post1304030\">my Byzantine Empire fan Dark Eras topic</a>. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <b>Bonus:</b> If I made anime opening to the Viking <b>Werewolf</b> game I run - it would look like this... ( Just change Alexios for PCs or Irene. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> )<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QRRQQVK6nV8\" data-code=\"QRRQQVK6nV8\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/QRRQQVK6nV8/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 03-29-2020, 03:30 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-29T23:21:09-07:00","id":1377296,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1376969-real-life-history-controversies#post1376969\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Like topic states. My controversial opinion - <b>Renaissance started in 9th century Byzantine Empire.</b> Seeing how Italian city-states of 15th century were based on long cultural and political relation with Constantinople. And how New Rome was heart of cultural heritage of Greek and Roman civilizations. It seems to come from logical implications.</div> </div> </div> </div>I took some Renaissance classes when I was an undergrad and you're basically right, that's exactly what I remember being taught so I don't know that it's all that controversial.<br /> <br /> When the Western Roman Empire fell, learning and knowledge in western Europe generally collapsed (compared to the earlier Roman-era). Meanwhile the Eastern Roman Empire basically kept right on trucking and Eastern Europe never really had a &quot;dark age&quot; in quite the same way that Western Europe did. A lot of the documents from Greek/Roman authors and such could still be found in the Eastern Roman Empire/Byzantium and a lot of that knowledge wasn't really &quot;lost&quot; in that part of the world. The Italian city states ended up functioning as the trade route between much of the Byzantine Empire and the rest of Europe and when Constantinople was sacked by the Crusaders a lot of that learning and knowledge, old documents, etc, ended up in Italy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-03T00:29:52-07:00","id":1378200,"title":"","content":"Interesting. I never thought of this. Thanks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-20T13:40:58-08:00","id":1470953,"title":"","content":"That depends on how you define the Renaissance. Strictly speaking, it <b>is</b> about Western Europe, and that's why it starts at the 15th century. In the east things went a lot different, not only on the Byzantine Empire, but also the Muslim states where Greek philosophers got to be intensely studied.<br /> <br /> But while the prevalence of educational institutions dwindled, there are many things that happened and improved in the Middle Ages, even the Early Middle Ages, that just didn't got recognized for a long time. Another feature of the Western Renaissance, and the reason I personally don't like to equate it to what happened in the East, was precisely the creation and spread of rumors and legends about how bad things were in the Middle Ages, because the Humanist philosophers of the time had and wanted to spread as low a vision on the Church as possible.<br /> <br /> In truth, the Western Europe Charlemagne found was already more technologically advanced than the Western Romans ever achieved to be. Which makes sense, as their political structure mostly (but not completely) failed, but their knowledge and technology, contrary to Renaissance belief, mostly got through and continued, precisely because the Church of the time invested <b>a lot</b> into keeping knowledge and technology going.<br /> <br /> So this belief, more than anything else, is what makes the Renaissance, because it is the reason they even chose the name. They firmly believed the Fall of Rome was kind of the &quot;death of civilization&quot;, and they were its &quot;resurrection&quot;. This isn't true (and they botched it with the statues).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-20T13:45:09-08:00","id":1470954,"title":"","content":"I think the only real &quot;controversial&quot; part of this is the word &quot;started&quot; in the bold of the first post. History is always a continuum. Breaking up history into periods and eras and whatever is always artificial and arbitrary. &quot;When did X period start?&quot; and &quot;when did Y period end?&quot; are always debated topics because real life doesn't unfurl with such neat and tidy milestones.<br /> <br /> As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar741_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"741\" data-vbnamecard=\"741\">AnubisXy</a> said, it's mainstream historical thought that there is a direct continuity between the Eastern Roman Empire, the Byzantine Empire, and the transfer of thought west in Europe via the Italian city states in the period called the Renaissance. We call it that name for a reason: it was a revival, not an innovation. The period between the First and Fourth Crusade is sometimes called a Byzantine renaissance because the first crusades helped rebuild Byzantium's control over Asia Minor as part of the deal to get western European forces to Jerusalem, which seemed like a resurgence of Byzantine power.<br /> <br /> What there isn't is a direct continuity between Byzantine culture and the Italian Renaissance. As much as we can draw lines between history, there's a line to be drawn between them. However much influenced by Byzantine culture, the Italian Renaissance was it's own thing, not Italy becoming Byzantine.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}